WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.859
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Thursday, October twenty ninth.

2
00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:12.487
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:15.544
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:15.544 --> 00:00:17.000
We start in Spain.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:26.393
Reuters reports at least sixty eight people were killed and dozens injured when two high speed trains collided near Urda, in Toledo province, on the line between Madrid and Andalusia.

6
00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:38.669
Australia's Smartraveller says train services between Madrid, Ciudad Real and Andalusia are suspended, and that clashes between police and protesters in Madrid have caused transport disruptions.

7
00:00:38.669 --> 00:00:44.596
Reuters reports far right groups clashed with police during a protest demanding the government's resignation.

8
00:00:44.596 --> 00:00:45.096
The U.S.

9
00:00:45.096 --> 00:00:51.624
Embassy has shared phone numbers from the train companies for families seeking information on passengers.

10
00:00:51.624 --> 00:00:57.342
In Yemen, Reuters reports the Houthis struck Aden International Airport after issuing an evacuation warning.

11
00:00:57.342 --> 00:01:00.992
The Institute for the Study of War also reports the strike.

12
00:01:00.992 --> 00:01:09.436
The Yemeni outlet South24 reports the transport minister announced this morning that airport operations have resumed.

13
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:10.779
In Saudi Arabia, the U.S.

14
00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:15.769
State Department issued a new alert on Houthi hostilities, including attacks on civilian airports.

15
00:01:15.769 --> 00:01:31.475
It says King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh announced a partial reopening on October twenty third for domestic flights and some international flights by national carriers, and that some flights are operating from Prince Naif bin Abdulaziz International Airport in Qassim.

16
00:01:31.475 --> 00:01:39.624
The State Department urges extreme caution at Saudi airports and says private car, train and bus options may provide alternatives.

17
00:01:39.624 --> 00:01:49.680
Separately, the Institute for the Study of War and the Critical Threats Project assess that Russia is giving the Houthis satellite imagery of Saudi oil and military infrastructure.

18
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.003
On sanctions, the U.S.

19
00:01:51.003 --> 00:02:03.201
Treasury Department sanctioned Rwanda's Ministry of Defense and the Zigama Credit and Savings Society for supporting armed groups in eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, including the M twenty three movement.

20
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:11.150
Treasury also sanctioned ten people and one entity over North Korean government I T worker schemes that defraud U.S. businesses.

21
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:27.356
In the Horn of Africa, the U.N. humanitarian office says intercommunal violence in Moyale has displaced thousands in Ethiopia's Oromia and Somali regions, and that up to fifty thousand people have crossed into Kenya since October twenty second, according to local authorities.

22
00:02:27.356 --> 00:02:29.842
And an update on Ebola travel rules.

23
00:02:29.842 --> 00:02:30.382
The U.S.

24
00:02:30.382 --> 00:02:40.787
Centers for Disease Control and Prevention amended its order requiring travelers to the United States to attest they have not been in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in the previous twenty one days.

25
00:02:40.787 --> 00:02:46.403
The amendment says the requirement also applies to aircraft crew, including on cargo flights.

26
00:02:46.403 --> 00:02:48.708
It still takes effect November third.

27
00:02:48.708 --> 00:02:55.905
Reuters reports airline groups have urged the agency to reconsider the requirements.

28
00:02:55.905 --> 00:02:57.966
Now a few quick notes by region.

29
00:02:57.966 --> 00:02:59.780
In Latin America, the U.S.

30
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:08.440
State Department says Ecuador has announced a nightly curfew from midnight to five a.m. in five provinces, including Guayas, from November first to November tenth.

31
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:16.028
In Mexico, the newspaper El Sol de Sinaloa reports armed men abducted a couple from a sushi restaurant in Culiacán.

32
00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:28.572
In Africa, the Nigerian outlet Dawn Herald reports suspected kidnappers abducted six people, including two secondary school students, from an estate in the Millennium City area of Kaduna on Tuesday, October twenty seventh.

33
00:03:28.572 --> 00:03:35.902
ACLED reports opposition forces in Somalia are tightening their grip around Baidoa, capturing key routes and districts.

34
00:03:35.902 --> 00:03:43.645
Canada's travel advisory service says rallies and demonstrations could occur around South Africa's municipal elections on November fourth.

35
00:03:43.645 --> 00:03:54.075
In South Asia, Afghan outlet Amu Television reports the Taliban Defense Ministry says four Pakistani drone strikes on Angoor Adda triggered Tuesday's border fighting, disputing Pakistan's account.

36
00:03:54.075 --> 00:04:00.253
KabulNow reports at least one civilian was killed and five injured.

37
00:04:00.253 --> 00:04:07.762
And Reuters reports at least six people were killed and power remains disrupted along India's eastern coast after Cyclone Arnab.

38
00:04:07.762 --> 00:04:16.980
In Europe, the Derry Journal reports police in Northern Ireland said a package delivered to a hotel at Ebrington Square in Derry on Tuesday, October twenty seventh, was a viable explosive device.

39
00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.196
Army experts made it safe, and police say dissident republicans are one line of inquiry.

40
00:04:21.196 --> 00:04:24.848
The U.S.

41
00:04:24.848 --> 00:04:34.641
State Department says unions have called a nationwide general strike in Italy from nine tonight through Friday evening, which may disrupt rail, air and local transport.

42
00:04:34.641 --> 00:04:44.834
And in cyber, The Record reports researchers say Belarus linked hackers are increasingly aligning their operations with Russian threat actors and Moscow's interests in Ukraine.

43
00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:46.315
That's today's brief.

44
00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:54.625
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

45
00:04:54.625 --> 00:05:03.060
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

46
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.296
This brief is digitally narrated.

47
00:05:05.296 --> 00:05:06.257
Questions?

48
00:05:06.257 --> 00:05:09.773
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

49
00:05:09.773 --> 00:05:11.852
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
